WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.306
"Is There Really Pus in Milk?"

00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:10.080
Dairy cows might normally live
for about 20 years,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.960
but they are typically
slaughtered for hamburger

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.720
after they're just
a few years old,

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.160
when they produce less milk
and their profitability drops.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.360
But another leading cause
of cow culling is mastitis,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.080
udder inflammation
and infections,

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.120
which affects 99.7 percent
of all dairy operations

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.920
in the United States.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.520
Because of the mastitis epidemic
in the U.S. dairy herd,

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.880
the dairy industry continues
to demand that American milk

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.200
retain among
the highest allowable

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.200
"somatic cell" concentration,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.440
nearly twice as much
as the rest of the world--

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.960
750,000 cells per milliliter.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.480
The concentration
in milk from healthy udders

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.240
should be less than 100,000.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.080
These somatic cells are mostly
white blood cells,

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.640
so, when you get up
to around 200,000,

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.160
which is, like,
a million per teaspoon,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.920
the udder is likely
to be infected.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.960
So, the somatic cell counts
in the bulk milk tanks

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.320
reflect the level of infection.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.040
Basically,
if the bulk milk tank,

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.960
which can hold
thousands of gallons,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.040
averages 200,000, then 15
percent of the contributing cows

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.720
are likely infected.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.640
At a concentration of 400,000,

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.920
which is more in line
with international standards,

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.720
a third of the cows
are likely infected.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.040
And staying within the 750,000
U.S. limit for Grade A milk

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.920
could reflect that
about two thirds of the cows

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.200
are infected.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.680
Now, somatic cells are not
synonymous with pus cells,

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.840
as has sometimes been
misleadingly suggested.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.240
Just as normal human blood
and breast milk

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.560
contain low levels
of white blood cells,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.640
so does milk from healthy cows.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.840
That's something we've known
for more than a century.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.480
The problem is that many
of our cows are not healthy.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.200
According to the latest
national government survey,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.400
about one in four dairy cows
in the United States

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.400
suffers from clinical mastitis,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.680
the second leading cause
of death on dairy farms.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.280
This is why the average somatic
cell concentration of U.S. milk

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.480
is that
of a likely infected udder,

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.360
at 204,000 cells per milliliter.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.240
How much actual pus
does that translate to?

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.280
Although the industry doesn't
like to talk about pus cells,

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:19.480
the fact that pus is present

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.840
in milk from inflamed
mammary glands is a given.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.520
But what constitutes
pus in milk?

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.800
The quote-unquote
"milk pus test" was introduced

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.040
more than a century ago.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.760
It pooled milk
from infected cows

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.080
that was estimated to contain
up to 2.5 percent pus by volume.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.320
And we'd really like to see
no more than, like, 0.1 percent.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.080
So, what does 204,000
cells per milliliter,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.360
the national U.S. average,
translate to?

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.880
That's a million cells
per teaspoon of milk.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.160
A million cells per spoonful
sounds like a lot,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.160
but pus is really concentrated.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.240
So, how much pus is there
in a glass of milk?

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.560
Not much.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:58.760
If you take the national average

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.800
and subtract the number
of white blood cells

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.280
you might see in normal milk,

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:05.280
you get about 100,000
inflammatory white blood cells

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.200
per milliliter,

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.640
or about 25 million per cup.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.720
Then it just depends on the
cellular concentration of pus.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.680
Although straight pus may have

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.000
80,000 cells per microliter or less,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:16.920
to be conservative,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.080
the most concentrated
I could find for straight pus

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.240
was about 150,000.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.680
Note that's per microliter,
not per milliliter.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.840
So, if there are about
25 million cells per cup,

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.280
and a microliter of pus
contains about 150,000 cells,

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.240
that would come out to be

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:34.400
about 150 microliters of pus
per glass of milk.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.120
There are about
50 microliters in a drop,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.200
so there'd only be a few drops
of pus per glass on average.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.600
And you can apparently taste
the difference.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.080
One of the major problems
associated with mastitis

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.600
is the flavor and texture
defects.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.640
Milk with high somatic
cell counts looks the same,

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.560
but evidently doesn't smell
or taste as good.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.760
The large amounts of pus
in mastitic milk

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.800
may be aesthetically
objectionable,

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.520
but it's important to note
this is not a food safety issue,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.480
thanks to pasteurization.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.120
The dairy industry emphasizes

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.920
that ingesting large amounts
of bovine white blood cells

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.160
has not been shown
to be harmful.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.960
No matter how inflamed
and infected udders get,

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:15.640
the pus gets cooked.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.640
But just as parents may not
want to feed their children

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:19.800
fecal bacteria in meat,

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.120
even if it's
irradiated fecal bacteria,

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.120
as the director of the
Food Policy Institute put it:

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.280
"Irradiated poop won't make you
sick, but it's still poop,"

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.920
parents may not want
to have their children sip

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.883
pasteurized pus.

